            "tag": "h3",
            "text": "In Memoriam"
          },
          {
            "tag": "p",
            "text": "The world lost an iconic fashion designer as well as a pioneering mathematician this past week."
          },
          {
            "tag": "p",
            "text": "American mathematician Gladys West , known for her work in the development of the Global Positioning System (GPS), died Saturday at age 95. West was hired by the U.S. Naval Proving Ground in 1956, and in 1978 she was named project manager for the Seasat project, an experimental surveillance satellite designed to provide data on a wide array of oceanographic features. This led to GEOSAT, a satellite programmed to create computer models of Earth’s surface, which allows GPS to make accurate calculations."
          },
          {
            "tag": "p",
            "text": "The Italian fashion designer known for his trademark red garments and “jet-set chic” style, died Monday at age 93. Born Valentino Clemente Ludovico Garavani, he sparked his fashion empire in 1959, when he presented his first collection and later that year designed a strapless cocktail dress in a red hue. He went on to include at least one red dress in each of his collections. Elizabeth Taylor , in Rome shooting Cleopatra , placed her order for a white column dress, which she wore to the premiere of Spartacus , and Valentino’s fate was sealed. He would go on to build a fashion powerhouse, which was sold in 1998 for $300 million."
          },
